/**
 * @addtogroup Foundation_BuildGuide_KnownLimitations
 * @{
 *
 * This page records limitations that affect Foundation 1.x consumers and
 * future release decisions. It complements the
 * @ref Foundation_BuildGuide_SupportMatrix "Support Matrix": the matrix
 * classifies targets, while this page explains boundaries that apply across
 * targets and workflows.
 *
 * A limitation remains listed until evidence removes it. A preset, successful
 * configure, or compiled archive is not sufficient evidence by itself.
 *
 * @section FoundationKnownLimitationsRelease Release and License Status
 *
 * - Foundation 1.2.0 is the current published release. Version 1.1.0 was the
 *   first published release; the planned 1.0.0 tag was superseded.
 * - Foundation is currently proprietary and grants no permission for external
 *   use, compilation, modification, integration, or redistribution without a
 *   separate written authorization.
 * - The final license, ownership, contribution, and distribution model require
 *   review by qualified legal counsel. This blocks any external-use grant; it
 *   is not a documentation disclaimer that can be waived by a build option.
 * - The source repository, examples, generated documentation, and exported
 *   artifacts all remain subject to the current `LICENSE` notice.
 * - The preparation checklist and unresolved ownership/licensing questions are
 *   tracked in @ref Foundation_Workflows_LegalReview. That record is not legal
 *   advice and does not replace review by qualified counsel.
 *
 * @section FoundationKnownLimitationsValidation Validation Coverage
 *
 * On 2026-09-25, tagged run
 * [36194693563](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/Foundation/actions/runs/36194693563)
 * built and published Foundation 1.2.0 packages after native Debug and Release
 * testing and isolated package-consumer validation. Native main-branch run
 * [36194411008](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/Foundation/actions/runs/36194411008)
 * passed macOS Arm64, Linux GCC/Clang x64, and Windows MSVC/Clang x64.
 * Clean local macOS 15.3.2 Arm64 runs additionally passed 162/162 tests
 * in both Debug and Release, followed by the isolated Release package consumer.
 * Main-branch run
 * [36227959990](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/Foundation/actions/runs/36227959990)
 * also passed the full native matrix, including Linux Arm64 GCC and Clang,
 * warnings as errors, sanitizers, coverage, and the feature-option matrix.
 * Embedded run
 * [36227959989](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/Foundation/actions/runs/36227959989)
 * built, exported, and inspected AVR and every declared Arm profile.
 * Clean macOS Arm64 and Rosetta 2 x64 validation additionally executed every
 * native example and the interactive launcher. All 12 sketches compile with
 * the stock Arduino AVR core, and a package-consuming firmware passed 14/14
 * runtime checks on an Arduino Uno.
 * Main-branch run
 * [36375583593](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/Foundation/actions/runs/36375583593)
 * subsequently executed and compared all Debug, Release, and exported examples
 * with Linux GCC/Clang x64 and Windows MSVC/Clang x64. Both Windows jobs also
 * passed scripted `.cmd` launcher scenarios; Linux exports correctly contained
 * no launcher.
 *
 * The following evidence is still pending:
 *
 * - manual opening of the example launcher through Windows Explorer;
 * - Raspberry Pi-specific consumer and hardware evidence;
 * - Arm/STM32 firmware linkage and target execution beyond hosted package
 *   cross-compilation;
 * - representative PSoC product-toolchain integration.
 *
 * Docker may reproduce Linux and some cross-compilation environments. It does
 * not substitute for native macOS, native Windows/MSVC, or physical embedded
 * target evidence. See @ref Foundation_Workflows_ReleaseV1 for the individual
 * validation gates.
 *
 * @section FoundationKnownLimitationsPlatforms Platform Scope
 *
 * Rows marked `RELEASE CANDIDATE` or `PUBLISHED` in the support matrix are not
 * `SUPPORTED` until their full acceptance evidence passes. PSoC 5LP and
 * generic Arm profiles are experimental. PIC is explicitly deferred and has no current preset, package, or
 * compatibility promise.
 *
 * Cross-compiled archives do not provide a complete firmware environment.
 * Foundation does not supply a target C runtime, startup code, interrupt
 * vectors, CMSIS, vendor HAL, device definitions, memory layout, linker script,
 * flashing workflow, or board support package. The consuming firmware owns
 * those components and must match the archive's compiler, CPU, ABI, FPU, and
 * runtime assumptions.
 *
 * @section FoundationKnownLimitationsPackages Package Boundaries
 *
 * - Exported packages contain one target-specific Release static library.
 *   Debug libraries remain source-build artifacts and are not distributed.
 * - There is no universal/fat package, runtime architecture dispatch, shared
 *   library, system installer, or package-manager publication in version 1.x.
 * - Packages with different compilers, standard-library ABIs, CPU flags, or
 *   feature selections are not interchangeable and must not be merged.
 * - The repository package-consumer fixture currently validates the default
 *   feature set, including Math and Time. The complete feature-option package
 *   matrix remains tracked as `PKG-002`.
 * - Direct source inclusion is available for Arduino-style and internal use,
 *   but the exported CMake package is the primary Foundation 1.x desktop
 *   consumer contract.
 *
 * @section FoundationKnownLimitationsLanguage Runtime and API Boundaries
 *
 * - CMake package and direct-source builds require a C++17-capable compiler;
 *   C++14 and older are rejected. Arduino source builds accept C++11, where
 *   relaxed-`constexpr` mutators are ordinary inline functions and the `_v`
 *   TypeTraits variables are unavailable. Foundation avoids requiring the
 *   complete C++ standard library.
 * - TypeTraits intentionally implements the subset used and documented by
 *   Foundation; it is not a complete replacement for `<type_traits>`.
 * - Containers and TaskScheduler use caller-owned, fixed-capacity storage.
 *   They do not allocate, resize, own, or destroy the supplied arrays.
 * - Callback is allocation-free and does not own bound objects. Bound objects,
 *   contexts, clocks, tasks, and external storage must outlive their users.
 * - TimePoint ordering and Duration arithmetic use modular half-range rules.
 *   Intervals at or beyond the selected tick type's half-range are ambiguous or
 *   invalid by contract.
 * - Heap-backed dynamic matrices depend on the target runtime's allocation
 *   backend. Allocation failure is reported without exceptions; attached
 *   caller-owned storage is the deterministic alternative.
 *
 * Consult the relevant module documentation for the exact ownership, invalid
 * state, aliasing, rollover, and exception contracts.
 *
 * @section FoundationKnownLimitationsQuality Quality Gates Still Open
 *
 * The inventoried Math and remaining-module behavior gaps are closed. Every
 * enabled public header compiles independently; supported native compilers
 * enforce warnings as errors; native sanitizers pass; and the GCC coverage job
 * enforces the agreed 90% line threshold. Remaining quality work concerns the
 * platform, downstream-consumer, controlled-failure, and hardware evidence
 * listed above. Examples demonstrate API use and are never counted as tests.
 *
 * The maintained release scope is summarized in the repository
 * `CHANGELOG.md`. Update this page, the changelog, support matrix, and release
 * backlog together whenever evidence changes one of these statements.
 *
 * @}
 */
